NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON IN RE WHIRLPOOL
1. As you review the opinion, consider each of the requirements for class certification under Rule 23(a). Is numerosity under Rule 23(a)(1) met? Why? Is commonality under Rule 23(a)(2) met? What are the common questions? How about typicality (Rule 23(a)(3)) and adequacy (Rule 23(a)(4))?

As our next case, Wal-Mart v. Dukes, reveals, one of the most difficult questions at the class certification stage can be commonality. In this regard, consider the following formulation of the commonality inquiry by Ben Spencer, who describes a three-step approach for identifying common questions:

(1) assume each plaintiff proceeds separately; (2) identify all legal and factual questions that would arise in the process of adjudicating each claim; and (3) determine whether there is a question that would recur in each action.
